---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import RtlContinuity from "../components/RtlContinuity.astro";
import SnapshotTiming from "../components/SnapshotTiming.astro";
import KnownApplications from "../components/KnownApplications.astro";
import BrowserSession from "../components/BrowserSession.astro";
import InteractiveDemo from "../components/InteractiveDemo.astro";
import { url, evidenceUrl, evidenceAsset } from "../lib/specs";
import experiments from "../data/experiments.json";
import work from "../data/work.json";
const byId = new Map(work.items.map(item => [item.id, item]));
const active = experiments.filter(item => item.status !== "deferred" && !byId.get(item.id)?.archived);
const activeWork = work.items.filter(item => active.some(experiment => experiment.id === item.id));
const taskCount = activeWork.reduce((n, item) => n + item.total, 0);
const completed = activeWork.reduce((n, item) => n + item.done, 0);
const acceptedCount=work.items.filter(item=>item.archived).length;
---
<Base title="The radio you already own">
  <section class="hero">
    <div><p class="kicker"><span class="live-dot"></span> OPEN HARDWARE RESEARCH / COMMITTED EVIDENCE</p><h1>The radio<br />you <em>already own.</em></h1><p class="lede">An ESP32 receiver experiment, an RTL-SDR that already delivers samples, and FPGA routes with real limits. Actual BLE packets and FM station data now reach the host. We’re measuring where these receivers work and where their limits matter.</p><div class="actions"><a class="primary-link" href={url("work/")}>Follow the experiments ↗</a><a class="text-link" href={url("compare/")}>See the receiver measurements →</a></div></div>
    <RtlContinuity />
  </section>
  <div class="fact-strip"><div><small>ESP32 / IDENTIFIED</small><strong>ESP32-D0WD-V3</strong><span>Rev. 3.1 · 4 MiB flash · CP2102</span></div><div><small>ORIGINAL IMAGE / PRESERVED</small><strong>Two matching full reads</strong><span>Matching boot after user-reported power cycle</span></div><div><small>RTL-SDR / MEASURED</small><strong>Four 65-second trials</strong><span>One reported gap at 2.048 MS/s</span></div><div><small>ESP-SDR / MEASURED TRANSPORT</small><strong>600/600 CRC + count passes</strong><span>Five known BLE packets decoded</span></div></div>
  <InteractiveDemo />
  <section class="section"><div class="section-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">01 / THE CURRENT CHECKPOINT</p><h2>Preserved. Restored. Delivering snapshots.</h2></div><p>Snapshot transport is repeatable in this series. Continuous delivery and controlled RF response are separate proof gates.</p></div><div class="card-grid"><article class="research-card"><p class="eyebrow">PHYSICAL FLASH READ / WRITE</p><h3>The fallback survives a trial</h3><p>Two independent 4,194,304-byte reads match. After all radio trials, a final full restored readback matches those original bytes, and a fresh reset boot again shows the historical GPIO test. The board currently runs that original firmware.</p><p>The user confirms power removal and reapplication. A matching original application boot and independent review now complete recovery acceptance, with UART-reset and electrical-measurement limits explicit.</p><a href={evidenceUrl("docs/evidence/firmware-preservation/README.md")}>Preservation →</a> · <a href={evidenceUrl("docs/evidence/user-power-cycle-recovery/README.md")}>Restoration →</a></article><article class="research-card"><p class="eyebrow">PHYSICAL UART / DIAGNOSTIC</p><h3>Baud rate is part of the experiment</h3><p>The upstream 2 Mbaud image booted, but Linux clamped this classic CP2102 bridge to 1 Mbaud. A 1 Mbaud variant also booted without protocol replies.</p><p>An instrumented 115,200-baud build completes initialization and answers INFO, SYNC, BAUD?, CAPS and LIMITS?. It is a diagnostic image. During the SDR trial, a clean 921,600-baud variant answered complete queries and delivered the measured snapshot baseline. The original GPIO firmware is now restored.</p><a href={evidenceUrl("docs/evidence/sdr-diagnostic-trial/README.md")}>Read the successful diagnostic →</a></article><article class="research-card"><p class="eyebrow">HOST TOOLS / PHYSICAL GATES OPEN</p><h3>Integrity passes. Timing constrains applications.</h3><p>Pinned firmware manifests, a CRC-checking capture tool, a browser spectrum bridge and a bounded BLE source/decoder are implemented and tested. The physical baseline now records 100 full-size snapshots in all six rate/format combinations.</p><p>All 600 pass CRC and counts. Median command-to-payload is about 361 ms for 8-bit and 451 ms for 10-bit. The first 1,024-bin browser stream failed CRC after 40.88 seconds; that failure is retained. A second 512-bin browser session completed 60.005 seconds with 7,205 CRC-checked frames. Five owned BLE packets now decode correctly. A physical Bluetooth monitor now verifies accepted source settings. Initial finite source diagnostics yielded no termination; later timer trials report zero completed events. A 262-snapshot RF discriminator is inconclusive, so capture hit rates remain unverified. Calibrated RF characterization remains open. AtomVM is deferred.</p><a href={url("work/?work=the-board-captures-repeatable-radio-snapshots")}>Capture acceptance gate →</a> · <a href={evidenceUrl("docs/evidence/ble-counted-source-smoke/README.md")}>Source counter limitation →</a></article></div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="section-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">02 / OPEN EXPERIMENTS</p><h2>{active.length} active evaluations. {acceptedCount} accepted.</h2></div><p>Active work records {completed}/{taskCount} checked tasks. Recovery, snapshot transport and receiver comparison are archived; AtomVM is deferred. Counts come from committed OpenSpec and each requirement retains its scope.</p></div><div class="card-grid">{experiments.map(item => { const record = byId.get(item.id); return <article class="research-card"><span class="card-number">{String(item.number).padStart(2,"0")}</span><span class="pill">{item.status === "deferred" ? "Deferred" : record?.archived ? "Accepted" : record?.lane === "planned" ? "Planned" : record?.lane === "verification" ? "Verification" : "In progress"}</span><h3>{item.short}</h3><p>{item.summary}</p><div class="card-bottom"><span>{item.status === "deferred" ? "Excluded from this goal" : `${record?.done ?? 0}/${record?.total ?? 0} tasks`}</span><a href={`${url("work/")}?work=${item.id}`}>Inspect proposal ↗</a></div></article>; })}</div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="section-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">MEASURED / ORIGINAL ESP32 SNAPSHOTS</p><h2>Good payloads. Very small listening windows.</h2></div><p>At 80 MS/s, the nominal time coverage was 0.0565% for 8-bit and 0.0451% for 10-bit output in this series. UART transfer dominates.</p></div><SnapshotTiming compact /><p class="calc-note">The clean baseline requests 2412 MHz, a 20 MHz filter, hardware AGC and 16,380 pairs. These are settings, not calibrated bandwidth or known-source proof. <a href={evidenceUrl("docs/evidence/sdr-installation-uart921600/README.md")}>Installation and protocol replies</a> · <a href={evidenceUrl("docs/evidence/spectrum-baseline-1024-failed/README.md")}>Failed first browser session</a>.</p></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="section-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">MEASURED / LIVE SPECTRUM SESSION</p><h2>A real minute of display. Gaps in every frame.</h2></div><p>The bounded 512-bin profile passes its display and integrity gate. This is separate from controlled RF validation and from the failed first profile.</p></div><BrowserSession /></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="section-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">03 / VERIFIED RECEIVER APPLICATIONS</p><h2>Known packets. An identified station.</h2></div><p>Owned BLE data received by the ESP32, and actual no-FEC RDS station data received by the V4. Independent replays support both narrow results.</p></div><KnownApplications /><p class="calc-note"><a href={evidenceUrl("docs/evidence/evaluation-checkpoint/README.md")}>Current hardware state and inputs needed to finish the evaluations →</a></p></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="section-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">04 / CAPTURE BUDGET EXPLORER</p><h2>80 million samples. A fraction of a millisecond.</h2></div><p>A model from pinned upstream buffer limits. These are calculated ceilings, not measured ESP32 capture gaps or UART throughput.</p></div><div class="calculator"><div class="calc-controls"><label>ADC SAMPLE RATE<select id="sample-rate"><option value="80">80 MS/s</option><option value="40">40 MS/s</option><option value="16">16 MS/s</option></select></label><label>OUTPUT PRECISION<select id="sample-bits"><option value="10">10-bit I + 10-bit Q</option><option value="8">8-bit I + 8-bit Q</option></select></label><label>ASSUMED UART RATE<select id="uart-baud"><option value="921600">921,600 baud · baseline measured</option><option value="1000000">1,000,000 baud · no replies in trial</option><option value="115200">115,200 baud · diagnostic answered</option><option value="460800">460,800 baud · model only</option></select></label></div><div class="calc-results" aria-live="polite"><div><strong id="capture-duration">0.205 ms</strong><span>Radio time in one maximum snapshot</span></div><div><strong id="transfer-duration">444.3 ms</strong><span>Ideal payload transfer, 8N1</span></div><div><strong id="capture-duty">0.046%</strong><span>Ideal capture coverage ceiling</span></div></div><div class="calc-timeline" aria-label="Calculated acquisition window and transfer gap"><div id="capture-segment" class="capture"></div><div class="gap"></div></div><p class="calc-note">16,380 complex pairs. UART assumes ten wire bits per byte. Coverage = capture ÷ (capture + transfer). Packing, commands, processing and scheduling add time. The measured series above includes real transfer and processing overhead; this calculator remains an idealized model. Compact FFT output reduces data volume but does not remove acquisition gaps.</p><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/ESPARGOS/esp-sdr/blob/550fadea4d00a9e26ce921c5832167becb3dc20c/main/targets/esp32/receiver.c">Source: original ESP32 capture implementation ↗</a></div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="section-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">05 / WHY KEEP THE FPGA OPTION?</p><h2>Process small snapshots. Prove the link first.</h2></div><p>The exact Forgix revision, electrical interface and sustainable transport are physical gates. PCIe enumeration proves no DMA path.</p></div><div class="split"><article class="note-box"><h3>A bounded original-chip route</h3><p>The reviewed candidate copies and packs a completed ESP32 snapshot, then sends it through ordinary SPI DMA to an FPGA for counters, buffering, triggers or decimation. It preserves the capture gaps and needs measured SRAM access and transport integrity.</p><p>Forgix’s full-speed USB wire ceiling is 1.5 MB/s. Packed 80 MS/s × 20-bit samples need 200 MB/s before framing. The reviewed schematic connects PSRAM to the RP, not directly to the FPGA.</p><a href={url("fpga/")}>Open budgets, pin conflicts and source revisions →</a></article><article class="note-box"><h3>Choose work that fits the evidence</h3><p>Use the RTL-SDR for practical continuous HF/VHF/UHF receiver development, with application-specific antennas and tests. Use the ESP32 for short 2.4 GHz experiments: owned BLE packets work, while repeatability, event counts and RF calibration need further measurement.</p><p>Continuous wideband reception would require a separately validated architecture; the upstream S3/FPGA design is not a firmware upgrade for this LX6. AtomVM integration is excluded from the active goal.</p><a href={url("sources/")}>Primary sources and limits →</a></article></div></section>
</Base>
<script>
  function calculate() {
    const rate = Number((document.querySelector("#sample-rate") as HTMLSelectElement).value) * 1e6;
    const bits = Number((document.querySelector("#sample-bits") as HTMLSelectElement).value);
    const baud = Number((document.querySelector("#uart-baud") as HTMLSelectElement).value);
    const capture = 16380 / rate;
    const transfer = Math.ceil(16380 * bits * 2 / 8) * 10 / baud;
    const duty = capture / (capture + transfer);
    document.querySelector("#capture-duration")!.textContent = `${(capture * 1000).toFixed(3)} ms`;
    document.querySelector("#transfer-duration")!.textContent = `${(transfer * 1000).toFixed(1)} ms`;
    document.querySelector("#capture-duty")!.textContent = `${(duty * 100).toFixed(3)}%`;
    (document.querySelector("#capture-segment") as HTMLElement).style.width = `${duty * 100}%`;
  }
  document.querySelectorAll(".calc-controls select").forEach(select => select.addEventListener("change", calculate));
  calculate();
</script>
